- P-12 **After the switch-over, walk runs the compiled type checker on its programs, and then uses the static coercions that the checker inserts.** The curator wants the checker usable on the programs that walk runs. Today `fortress typecheck` checks a program only against the compiler's prelude, so it is no check of walk's programs (F-9 holds this fact). Walk is not changed for this before the switch-over. After it, walk runs the checker on its programs and so gains their static types. Walk then uses the static coercions that the checker inserts, in place of its choice on the run-time value (P-59, walk's choice of coercions until the switch-over). Row NEW-1: walk runs no static type check, so it has no static types and cannot use the coercions that the checker inserts.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-12"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read of POSITIONS found the unbuilt step without a row; the drafted row named (row NEW-1, filed by the coordinator), walk's branch of Shell.java:420-424 and StaticChecker.java:166 at d4d454f9d; rows 69 and 19 found and not the gap

- P-17 **Unboxed doubles and arrays of unboxed doubles are required: without them the revival misses its point.** Why: a language meant for high performance cannot box its numbers. Today the code generator boxes every number: an `RR64` value is an object of the class `FRR64` (row NEW-2). In Java models of microGPT's three array kernels, the dot product, the matrix product and the row lift, boxing is the main cost (F-11 holds the measurement). How arrays store doubles, as `double[]`, is P-84's decision.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-17"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the boxing without a row; the drafted row named (row NEW-2); rows 306 and 302 hold the cost, not the gap

- P-25 **`Specification/` stays the standard, and the Types chapter of the team's 2012 restart is cited beside it.** The restart is a new specification that the team began in 2012 and left unfinished (F-166). Where its Types chapter, `Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick`, covers a topic, a worker cites it beside `Specification/` as the designers' later word. On the rules that the revival revises, such as the exclusion rule, the chapter is the designers' latest intent, which the goal is to finish, and `Specification/` is revised to follow it (P-26: the specification is revised to the team's later word). Three of the chapter's passages bear on the specification; P-97 holds how the revision uses the first two. It names the exclusion rule "instantiation exclusion" and gives it in two parts: which instantiations of a generic exclude each other (`types.tick:355-360`), and, in its note, a rule on trait declarations (`:361-376`). The revised `Specification/` takes that form: the first part in its chapter on types, the second in its chapter on traits. It adds a `covariant` modifier for a trait's static parameters, which neither path implements and which is future work, off the path to microGPT (P-62). Third, it says that `Bottom` cannot be written in a program, as `Specification/basic/types-vals-vars.tex` already says of `BottomType`.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-25"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the Bottom passage counted among P-97's; P-97 holds the first two passages only, and Specification/basic/types-vals-vars.tex:106-107 already says that BottomType cannot be written; the three topics are the coordinator's ask of 2026-09-26T07:56:16.747Z, which his Agreed. of 08:01:03.475Z took

- P-30 **The check of microGPT under walk after each batch must take less than a minute.** A batch is a set of agents' changes that land together. The minute is the curator's goal for what the check runs: only about two forward-and-backward passes of the model, not a full training, with the results compared with reference values. That is enough to show that the model's mathematics still works, and that the library's changes, such as those to the arrays, did not break it. A slower check is to him a waste of time. The commit stage of each batch, the step that lands its changes, runs the check as `explorations/coordinator/tools/mg-run.sh`, the quick versions of microGPT's two programs at once, outside the gate.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-30"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found whose goal the minute is unclear and the batch's terms undefined; batch and commit stage defined; the minute said as his goal for what the check runs, his 2026-10-08T17:49:22.269Z (two passes, under a minute, the goal) and 16:20:06.948Z (not a full training); what follows a slower check is review-queue item 239's, not settled here

- P-32 **The record's maps are kept current and used, so that nothing that the record holds is worked out again from scratch.** The maps are `FACTS.md`, `INDEX.md` and the territory map, `explorations/coordinator/map/`. The territory map surveys the repository down to where the team wrote the intent of each design. Part of that intent is written only in the papers under `Papers/`, not in the specification. So the map covers the papers too, and shows where to look up a design's intent. The coordinator reads FACTS and `INDEX.md` whole when it starts a session, and gives each worker's brief a mission briefing from the territory map, not the whole map. The territory map's surveys date from 2026-09-16 (its README), so a status or a line number in them can be stale.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-32"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the coordinator skill's parts and the word rung, which a worker does not have; said as what the coordinator does, coordinator SKILL.md:218 and references/batch.md:44, and his 2026-09-27T06:31:31.170Z (a mission briefing from the map); who keeps the territory map current is review-queue item 240's, not settled here

- P-33 **The work toward microGPT compiled follows the phases, in the order that the curator approved.** These phases are steps of the work, not the compiler's phases, and they are numbered 3 to 6, after two earlier ones. Phase 3, the compiled checker reports no error on the interpreter's whole library (the distance to the switch-over at zero; F-53 defines it and holds its value); phase 4, the switch-over, first the natives, the library's `builtinPrimitive` bindings made to run on the compiled path, then the compiled path reads the interpreter's library and the compiler's prelude is deleted; phase 5, microGPT compiles: its static types, the array design and the code generator's open gaps; phase 6, microGPT runs fast. Code-generator work goes on beside that order, under either library (P-37). Row 320, an api implemented by a component of another name does not link, is part of the switch-over's design.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-33"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the row 320 paragraph without a ruling, history in the entry, and phase numbers that clash with the compiler's phases; the agent's gating of row 320's open half goes (F-22 states it; review-queue item 36), the repair batch's sentence goes as history, and his approval of 2026-09-26T09:04:41.396Z, which put row 320's open half into the switch-over's design, stays; the phases said as steps of the work

- P-39 **Every batch's gate measures the checker count, and its total never turns the gate red.** A batch is a set of tasks, each called a rung, that agents build at once and land together. Its gate runs the gate's steps on their merged tree, and then the checker count, as step 8 (`explorations/coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.md`); the gate of `fortress-repo`'s gate part has no such step. The checker count and the distance to the switch-over both count the compiled checker's errors on the interpreter's library. The count stops where the checker crashes or meets an error that it cannot get past; the distance counts every error, over all apis and components (F-53 defines both and holds their values). A rise of the count's total is reported and never fails a batch on its own. The count's stage can still turn the gate red on the faults that F-66 names. One is a checker crash that no rung of the batch listed in advance as expected, in the field `expectedCheckerCrash` of the batch's manifest, the part of the batch's script that declares each rung's task. The gates of batches whose work drives the distance down also measure the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the distance's full measurement takes far longer than the count, and a batch pays for it only when the distance is the number that it tries to move.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-39"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found step 8 of the batch's gate against the skill's seven-step gate, and rung and manifest undefined; batch, rung, the batch's gate and the manifest defined from climb-batch-workflow.md, sections Gate and The manifest and the scatter; fortress-repo references/gate.md:3

- P-40 **Sizes are checked, and a size that inference leaves unknown is an error only where it is used.** A size is a `nat` or `int` static parameter, such as the length of an array in its type. The compiled checker checks sizes, and refuses a size beyond `NN32` or `ZZ32` (F-140, the bound on both paths). Wherever the array design, the compiled path's design for arrays (P-85), creates a JVM array, it checks the length there, at run time, and refuses one over 2^31-1. A size that inference leaves unknown is an error only when it reaches a type, a name or a value. So a declaration may declare a size that none of its types uses, and pass. A call that cannot fix a size of the declaration that it would choose is refused, also inside an overload set, instead of passing over that declaration. In the rule that no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait, two instantiations differ when any static argument but an operator differs, so `Vec[\3\]` and `Vec[\4\]` are two; boolean arguments count too once the checker has boolean parameters. A size used as a number, as in the range `0#n`, converts to `ZZ32` where one is needed, as a numeral does. The 17 vector and matrix operators of the `FortressLibrary` api declare only the sizes that their types use, the change of `explorations/perf-probes/array-sites/e3-rebased.patch`. It finishes the team's own fix of 2008, which removed those sizes from the component only. The JVM length check waits for the array design; under walk such an array dies with a raw Java exception (row 527). The checker still passes over a declaration whose unfixed size occurs in its own parameter type (row 400). The checker compares no boolean arguments (row 406).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-40"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the patch's state unclear and the array design undefined; the patch is in the tree (e487cec2d; F-35), said in the present; the array design defined by P-85

- P-45 **Overloaded declarations may differ in their static parameters.** The specification drops its sentence that forbids this, and keeps it as superseded text. It states instead the overloading model of the type group's 2011 paper (`Papers/Types/`), which the compiled checker already used. The checker keeps the paper's three rules: No Duplicates, Meet and Return Type. It checks the Return Type Rule over every instance of a generic declaration that the argument types allow, not over one guessed instance. One rule of the old sentence survives: if one generic declaration is more specific than another, their static parameters must line up position by position, as Java's rule for overriding a generic method requires. The compiled checker enforces it (F-75 gives its form and scope). Walk chooses among overloads by the parameter types that each declaration declares, not by the order in which they are written. At load, walk judges each pair of two generic declarations by the checker's rules on their declared parameter types. It does not refuse a pair only because none of their parameter types exclude, as when one declaration is more specific than the other. Walk and the checker must accept the same pairs. The library repairs, by declarations of its own, the overload sets that the checker refused, and no line of microGPT's two programs changes. Where walk or the checker still judges a pair against these rules: walk compares two functional methods of a generic trait or object on the instance that a call makes (F-26); both paths refuse the paper's own valid example (row 539); walk refuses two generic functional methods of a trait without static parameters whose declared domains lie one inside the other, and fails at a call on such a pair in a generic trait (rows 779 and 780); and walk and the checker disagree on four kinds of pair (rows 759 and 786 to 788).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-45"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the positional rule's form and enforcement unclear, and nested read as declared inside; the checker enforces it (OverloadingOracle.scala:154-175), its form and scope F-75's, the scope an agent's (review-queue item 60); rows 779 and 780 said by their claims

- P-46 **A conversion only makes a call possible: it never changes which declaration runs when one already fits.** The declaration that runs is chosen on the parameter types that each overload declares: first those that apply, then the most specific of them (P-45). A generic declaration applies if some type within its bounds takes the arguments. Only then is the chosen declaration instantiated by promotion: at the narrowest type into which its arguments coerce (P-74). So beside `op(a: Any, b: Any)`, `op[\T extends Number\](a: T, b: T)` applies to a call with a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64`, at `T = Number`. It is the more specific, so it runs, at `ZZ64`. Why: the curator wants fast generic code that a JVM can specialise. His own broader rule is not taken: to apply any conversion that reaches a specific declaration, and to fall back to a plain one only when none does. Walk and the compiled checker both infer static arguments with coercion, by the same promotion. The compiled path inserts the conversions statically. P-49 gives the order in which the checker tries a call. Row 537: the compiled dispatch between two generic declarations of one name with different bounds.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-46"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found walk's promotion an agent's choice with an unclear status; it goes, F-137 states it, review-queue item 85 holds it

- P-48 **A type parameter that a call's arguments do not fix takes its bound, never `Bottom`.** The arguments leave a parameter unfixed when it occurs only in the result type; when two arguments meet at it with no narrowest type into which both convert (a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64` have one, `ZZ64`, by P-74); or when dispatch, at run time, picks a generic declaration and must choose the instance to run. Its instance is then the intersection of its upper bounds (`Any` if it has none). At checking, that is narrowed by what the call's expected type (P-49 defines it) requires of the declared return type; at run time, by the static type that the checker gave the call. It is never `Bottom`, never the union of the arguments' types, and never the value's type alone. The rule sets the instance, not which declaration runs (P-46). Why: the rule is the team's later word, from the POPL 2019 paper on multiple dispatch, and the curator trusts the team's later work above his own understanding (P-26). The rule changes nothing in microGPT, whose types are concrete. The library keeps the team's declarations of `fail`, `builtinPrimitive` and `List`'s empty comprehension, whose type parameters have no written bound, without the `extends Object` that a batch had added. Under walk, which has no static types, dispatch takes the value's type. Row 538: a compiled dispatcher that carries the static type that the checker gave the call, so that a generic declaration reached only at run time gets this instance. Rows 587, 591 and 612: three cases in which walk still gives `Bottom`.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-48"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found always will against P-12, and row 591's Bottom an agent's choice with an unclear status; the reason stays (option 1 of 2026-09-29T16:52:08.446Z), the forecast goes; row 591's choice goes, F-141 states it, review-queue item 81 holds it

- P-49 **The compiled checker tries a call first by subtyping, without the expected type, and coerces its arguments only when that attempt is not kept.** The expected type is the type that the call's context requires. The checker's first attempt checks the call by subtyping alone, without the expected type. It keeps that attempt when its result converts to the expected type, by coercion if need be. If it does not keep that attempt, it tries coercion of the arguments, with the expected type. So a declaration that fits the arguments as they are is not passed over for one that coercion reaches. Why: in the curator's words, coercion should be tried when there are no matches.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-49"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the third attempt an agent's choice with an unclear status; the third and the fourth go, since his decision names two (review-queue item 2), and F-78 states the four

- P-54 **`QQ`'s `ceiling` and `truncate` keep `ZZ`, their declared result type, and raise an error at +∞, −∞ and 0/0.** The specification's paragraph on these methods said that they return the argument itself at those three values (`Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex`, "Rational Numbers"). That paragraph is revised. `floor`, `round` and the brackets ⌊x⌋ and ⌈x⌉ are built on `ceiling` and `truncate`, so they raise too. Why: the declared integer result and other languages win over the paragraph: Python's `math.floor(inf)`, for one, raises. The library raises `DivisionByZero`, as the revised paragraph says. After the switch-over, `QQ` follows the float ruling (P-68, on rounding the float types): `floor` and `ceiling` return `QQ` and pass the infinities through, and the brackets, `round` and `truncate` give `ZZ` (row NEW-3).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-54"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the later QQ change without a row (row 330 is the float types'); the drafted row named (row NEW-3); rung said as the time, after the switch-over, his 2026-10-09T07:28:28.361Z

- P-58 **Every native binding is a `builtinPrimitive` call naming a static helper.** A native binding is a library body that is exactly `builtinPrimitive("…")`, the library's own form, and it keeps that form. Its string is to name a static Java method in `nativeHelpers/`, the compiled path's helpers, such as `"com.sun.fortress.nativeHelpers.simpleIntArith.intLT"`, in place of a glue class of walk, which the compiled path cannot use (F-32). Walk is to load the named helper through a loader beside its glue classes. At the parse, before the checker, the compiled path is to rewrite each such body into a synthetic `import java` of the helper and a call, and to refuse a string that names no helper. The bindings move onto helpers step by step, and from the step that moves a binding, the compiled checker checks it against the helper's Java signature, not all at once at the switch-over. The other form, `import java` declarations written in the library itself, is not taken. Every binding still names a glue class. Row NEW-4: walk loads a binding's string only as a glue class. Row 309: the compiled path has no counterpart of `builtinPrimitive`.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-58"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found rung undefined, the checker's sentence two-way, and no row; the checking said as reviews/item35-natives-binding.md:111 gives it, under his option 1 of 2026-10-10T05:27:34.812Z; the plan sentence goes; rows named: NEW-4 (walk's loader, drafted) and 309 (the compiled half)

- P-60 **The specification reads a `comprises` clause at the level of values.** `trait S comprises { U, V }` says that every value of `S` is a value of `U` or of `V`. So another trait may stand between `S` and its listed types. The specification defines "covers" once: a type covers another when every value of the second is a value of the first. The Meet Rule gains a case for closed traits. Where the rule asks for a declaration at the meet of two overloads' parameter types, several declarations below both serve, if together they cover the meet. So a call whose argument's static type stands between closed traits and their listed types may have several most specific declarations. The call's type is the intersection of their return types, and the declaration that runs is unique only at run time. The compiled checker uses coverage only in its overloading rules, to accept an overload set and to justify a call's tie among several most specific declarations, and not in its general subtype test. Why: the proof addendum (`reviews/comprises-type-level-proof-addendum.md`) shows this sound for ground declarations, at calls that need no coercion. A ground declaration's types hold no type variable, and `G[\ZZ32\]` is ground. The addendum shows it for coverage used only in that way, and leaves any wider use to a review of its own. A call that needs a coercion is outside the proof too (F-76 gives what the compiled checker does there). A declaration with static parameters, whose instance dispatch must choose at run time, is outside the proof: one instance would have to meet every one of the return types at once. The typing by the intersection is not extended to it, and the specification states that boundary. Rows 543 and 551: the compiled checker gives no call an intersection type where any declaration of the family has static parameters, and walk checks no `comprises` clause declared outside the main component.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-60"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the addendum's ask inside the Why, the coercion case open and no row; the ask stated as part of his yes of 2026-09-29T11:25:37.094Z (Astra's points), the addendum's lines 186-192; the coercion boundary from the addendum's lines 14-25 and Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:716-719, the tree F-76's; rows 543 and 551 named

- P-73 **Under walk, fixed-width integer arithmetic raises `IntegerOverflow` when its result does not fit, on the unsigned `NN32` and `NN64` too.** The rule covers every operation whose result can leave its type, `^`, `CHOOSE` and `LCM` included (F-132 holds what is built and its tests). So a subtraction below 0 on `NN32` raises. Code that means to wrap uses the specification's wrapping operators ∔ (`DOTPLUS`), ∸ (`DOTMINUS`) and ⨰ (`DOTTIMES`), fully parenthesised, since the specification states their precedence only among the three. The specification's saturating operators, such as ⊞ (`BOXPLUS`), are not in the interpreter's library; they come in a later step of their own (row NEW-5). Why: this is the specification's rule, and the compiled path already raised the error for addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, negation and absolute value on `ZZ32` and `ZZ64`. The decision is walk's. On the compiled path the rule holds as the specification's (P-25, the standard), and the compiled powers depart from it (rows 523 to 525).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-73"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the decision's reach to the compiled path unclear and the saturating operators without a row; the decision said as walk's (the asks of 2026-09-24 and 2026-09-26), the compiled path's departures rows 523 to 525 (F-132); the drafted row named (row NEW-5)

- P-75 **`NN32` coerces into `RR64` implicitly, since every `NN32` value converts to `RR64` exactly.** Why: a conversion that fits is implicit, the rule of P-74 on mixed widths. With the coercion, an operator that both `RR64` and `ZZ64` declare is ambiguous on both paths for a `ZZ32` with an `NN32`, since neither `ZZ64` nor `RR64` coerces into the other. Without it, P-74's rule, the narrowest type into which both arguments coerce, gives `ZZ64`. Row 575: the coercion, which the tree lacks, with this conflict and its measurement. Each way to build it changes a decided behaviour, so the choice of a way is not a worker's (`fortress-repo`'s exploring part).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-75"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the coercion's state and a worker's part unclear; row 575 said as the coercion the tree lacks, and the choice of a way as no worker's, fortress-repo references/exploring.md:7-11

- P-78 **`SUM` and `PROD` are one generic reduction each over the library's algebra, in place of the team's catch-all over `Number`, and take their identity from the static argument.** `SUM` is generic over `AdditiveGroup` and `PROD` over `MultiplicativeRing`. A reduction written with generators writes its static argument where nothing at the call fixes the element type. The compiled checker fixes it from neither the generators, the body nor a declared type that receives the value, and refuses every such `SUM` or `PROD` that leaves it out (row 731, a gap of the checker). So every clause form of `SUM` and `PROD` writes it, as in `SUM[\ZZ32\][j <- 0#n] f(j)`. A sum without generators is written as before, with no static argument: `SUM e` over a collection `e`, and the dot product `x DOT y`. `BIG MAXN`, `BIG MINN` and `BIG MINMAXN`, the team's `Number`-typed big operators for the maximum, the minimum and both, each with an identity, are gone; no program called them. `MAX` and `MIN` get no identity on the integers or the floats, so an empty `BIG MAX` or `BIG MIN` raises `EmptyReduction` (F-60: a maximum's identity is its type's least value, which a coercion into a wider type does not carry). Under walk, an empty `SUM` or `PROD` over another algebra, such as a user `AdditiveGroup`, still fails (row 436) until the specification's `HasIdentity` is built.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-78"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the rule against the skill's every clause form; his answer 7, a clause form whose element type nothing at the call fixes writes its static argument, restored, and row 731's refusal and workaround said as the checker's gap

- P-79 **Every team demo is kept running as the tree changes.** The team demos are the programs in `ProjectFortress/demos/`; no suite or step of the gate runs them. If a change breaks a demo and the way to repair the demo is known, the batch that makes the change, the set of agents' changes that land together, also rewrites the demo. For example, the change that made `10^(-4)` an error (P-153, the integer power) also wrote `10.0^(-4)` in `BiCGSTAB.fss` and `BiCGSTAB2.fss`, and `10.0^(-6)` for `10^(-6)` in `npbft.fss`. A demo that fails for want of a feature is made to run again once the feature is built. So the demos known to fail today are to be respelled where the way is known: the five demos that walk refuses at load, since they leave an inherited abstract method without a body (row 670, which names them); `trips.fss`, which walk runs but the compiled checker refuses, since its `generator` object extends `StandardTotalOrder[\trip\]` without being a `trip` (row 710); the timing line of `BiCGSTAB.fss` and `BiCGSTAB2.fss`, which divides a `ZZ64` by a float and so stops walk (row 791); and the reductions that the compiled checker refuses because they leave out the static argument of a big operator whose bound names its own static parameter, such as `SUM`; their repair writes the argument (row 731).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-79"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found that nothing runs the demos and batch undefined; no suite or gate step runs them (build.xml, the worker skill), and batch defined

- P-84 **An `RR64` array is stored as a Java `double[]`, unboxed, and arrays keep the team's `nat` sizes in their types.** Why: a high-performance language cannot box its array elements, and the sizes are central to the team's design. So the storage and the sizes are one design: the sizes stay in the array types as `nat` static parameters, as the library's `Vector` and `Matrix` declare them, and the compiled checker checks them. The sizes do not wait while a first version of the store carries them only as run-time fields; that layered plan was rejected. Both halves follow the library's own patterns (P-27, the library's own practice is the standard). The compiled checker checks `nat` static parameters (F-67). The compiled path's `double[]` store (row NEW-6) waits with the other array decisions until after the switch-over; among them is decision A, how an `RR64` array made through the library's generic array traits gets that store (P-85).
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-84"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found decision A's wording unclear and the store without a row; decision A said as reviews/array-design-ways.md:8 gives it; the drafted row named (row NEW-6)

- P-85 **The array design's three questions are open, with no default, and return after the switch-over with the array decisions A, B and D.** The array design is the compiled path's design for arrays. The three questions: whether the array factory chooses a store for each element type; whether the store also keeps the size as a field, beside the size in the type; and whether a factory for a size known only at run time returns the unsized `Array[\E,I\]`, whose type holds no size. These questions are apart from decisions A, B and D: decision A is how an `RR64` array made through the library's generic array traits gets the `double[]` store (P-84), B is where unboxed arithmetic happens, and D is the sizes in the model's own types (P-87). The whole preparation for the array work is to be done before the switch-over; only the work itself waits.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-85"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found way 4a an agent's choice with an unclear status, and decision A's wording unclear; way 4a goes, F-270 states it, review-queue items 30 and 229 hold it; decision A said as in P-84; what the preparation covers is review-queue item 230's, not settled here

- P-88 **The model's vocabulary leaves `+`, `-` and `MAX` between an array and a scalar to the library's scalar-extension block, and its diagonal keeps its own product.** The vocabulary, the array operations that microGPT is written in, declares none of these six operators, an array with a number on either side; the library's scalar-extension block, in `Library/FortressLibrary.fss`, serves them. `Diag`, the vocabulary's diagonal matrix, extends `Matrix` and overrides `mul`, the method that the library's matrix product calls, so `diag(v) m` scales the rows of `m` without forming the matrix. This is what the type system promises: a diagonal is a kind of matrix, with a faster product of its own. Row 299 records what the general product costs on a diagonal: 13 to 54 times as much as scaling the rows. Since the diagonal is read-only, a read-only matrix trait, `ReadableArray2`, which `Array2` would extend as `Array1` extends `ReadableArray1`, is also wanted (row NEW-7): it is a library change, not a definition in the vocabulary.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-88"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the read-only matrix trait without a row; the drafted row named (row NEW-7), his page comment of 2026-10-21 23:29 UTC keeping the sentence

- P-96 **A change of the specification is shown in layers, as the team showed its own changes in the 1.0 release of 2008 and in the working draft of 2009.** This form is the revision form. A callout that prints in every build marks each changed passage; a `\note` will not do, since a release build hides it. Appendix I's revival section has an entry for each change: its reason, its original text, what a return to route C would mean for it, or that it would be the same under route C (P-95 says what route C is), and the decision that it follows. The front matter has one paragraph on the revision, and a document in the repository holds the full reasoning. Citations call the unrevised copy, `Specification-1.0-frozen/`, the Working Draft of February 2011: the copy is that draft byte for byte, and only its `fortress.1.0.pdf` is the 1.0 release (F-163). The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/specification.md` gives each part in full, including what else an Appendix I entry holds.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-96"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the S1 alias pointing at nothing and the route C item odd for other changes; the alias goes (no other entry says S1), and the item says what Appendix I's entries say where route C changes nothing

- P-151 **Only the `where`-clause forms that the team wrote down are built: conditional extension, a function's or a method's condition, and `AnyList`'s `comprises` clause.** A `where` clause states conditions on static parameters, and may introduce static variables of its own, as `where [\E\]` does. A trait or object that extends a type under a `where` condition is that type only where the condition holds, and its members are checked with the condition holding (conditional extension). A `where` condition on a function's or a method's own static parameters bounds them. The team's commented-out clause `AnyList comprises List[\E\] where [\E\]` becomes live, which clears the type error of `AnyList`'s join `||`; its other operations, `addLeft` and `addRight`, move into `List` (row NEW-8). `SUM`'s fusion with `MAX` and `MIN`, the library's fast path for the maximum or the minimum of sums, stays. A method's `where` condition clears four of the fusion's six type errors, and the team's commented-out generic `distribute`, after a measurement of it, the other two (F-242: as the team wrote it, it only moves the two errors; the form in the tree, with its result over its own `K`, clears them). Why: a feature with little support in the team's work bends the language beyond what the curator can review. He has such a feature built where the team left enough design intent for it, or where it is on the critical path to running fast compiled. Row 773: the members scoped to the condition, since a member checked under it stays callable where the condition fails. Row 774: walk's half of conditional extension. Row 701: the code generator's half.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "P-151"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: the cold read found the move of addLeft and addRight without a row and the distribute sentence against F-242; the batch's departure goes, F-243 states it, review-queue item 188 holds it; the drafted row named (row NEW-8); the distribute sentence keeps his option 3 of 2026-10-16 (measure the team's commented fix) and gives F-242's result, without the K form, which his HOLD of 2026-10-16T20:09:30.180Z left unanswered

- F-78 **The compiled checker instantiates a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes at the intersection of its upper bounds, never `BottomType`, and tries a call by subtyping without the expected type first.** It builds P-48 and P-49. Where the call has an expected type, its attempts come in this order: subtyping without the expected type, kept when its result converts to it; subtyping with the expected type; coercion with it; and coercion without it. The second lets a call whose untyped function argument only the expected type fixes type-check, since the coercion attempt cannot check such an argument (row 507). An argument of intersection type fixes a parameter at the whole intersection, not at one conjunct (row 541). The run time has no class for an intersection type, so such an instance dies as it loads (row 559). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*` and `InferContextKeepsFit`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 7; the order, by reading, `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/decision-record.md`, D4, and `scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala:733-737`.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "F-78"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: P-49's third and fourth attempts moved here (review-queue item 2): the order, by reading, decision-record D4 and Functionals.scala:733-737 at d4d454f9d

- F-137 **Under `walk`, a generic call's static arguments are inferred with coercion, by P-74's promotion, also for the declaration that an overloaded call chooses (P-46).** A lone type parameter is one that is the whole declared type of some argument and that no other argument fixes through a type such as `List[\T\]`. It takes the narrowest of its arguments' run-time types, their coercion targets and its bounds that admits every argument. So `ZZ32` with `ZZ64` gives `ZZ64`. `ZZ64` with `RR64` gives the declared bound, the only candidate that admits both, since a `ZZ64` goes into `RR64` only when written out (P-74). With no narrowest candidate, the parameter takes its bound where every argument is below the bound (F-141), and otherwise the arguments' join. Walk, which has no static types, promotes from the run-time types. So where a static type is wider than its value, the two paths instantiate differently (row 509). The rule does not reach these open defects: a big operator over a list of numerals with no static argument (row 432); a `nat` parameter used as a value at an `NN32` binding, refused at the binding (row 486); a static argument that only the call's result mentions, at a typed binding (row 510); and a varargs or tuple parameter over mixed widths (row 511). Nor does it reach, by inference and not measured, a numeral that fits `ZZ32` passed alone for a parameter whose bound a `ZZ32` neither extends nor coerces into, since walk reads such a numeral as a `ZZ32` (P-76). Gated by `tests/InferCoercionRungK.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-inference-walk/REPORT.md` sections 2 and 9.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "F-137"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: P-46's sentence on walk's promotion moved here: the reason added; a default goes, review-queue item 85 holding the review

- F-270 **The compiled checker opens a value of a trait whose `comprises` clause lists a type at a `where`-clause size: it gives each listed type's `nat` and `int` `where`-clause sizes a fresh name for the call.** In `trait NatParam comprises { N[\n\] } where [\ nat n \]`, a `NatParam` passed where `N[\$n\]` is expected, `$n` being the checker's inference variable for the called function's size parameter, binds `$n` to the fresh size, and the call's result type carries it. An opened size is not known to differ from any size. Walk's `comprises` check at load reads such a size as any size, the same at each of its places; at a call, walk binds the argument's own size. This is way 4a of fork 3 of the array design, a size known only at run time: the checker reads such a size as fixed for that call, not as unknown. P-85 leaves the array design's three questions open with no default; the third stands on this ground, and way 4a is the one way built on it. The code generator does not compile such a trait: "Can't compile TraitDecl NatParamT" (row 307; `compiler_tests/XXXNatOpenRun`). In row 682's program, with `trait NatParamT comprises { Nt[\n\] } where [\nat n\]`, the checker refuses `f(x: NatParamT)` beside `f[\nat n\](x: Nt[\n\])` as two declarations of one parameter type, where walk runs the pair (row 682, contested). F-251 holds the same opening for a type variable. Gated by `compiler_tests/NatOpenChecker`, and under walk by `tests/NatOpenWalk` and `NatOpenUnlistedExtender`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md` section 1, "Fork 3, way 4a, under Q13.3", and section 12.
  from: explorations/coordinator/consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/positions-followup/proposals.md, "F-270"; by: the POSITIONS follow-up writer (Opus); note: P-85's sentence on way 4a moved here: the conflict with P-85 stated; the agent's landing goes, review-queue items 30 and 229 holding the review
